##DESCRIPTION
## How does adding a number change the mean and standard deviation?
##ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Statistics)
## DBchapter(Exploratory data analysis/descriptive statistics)
## DBsection(Summary statistics)
## Institution(Fitchburg State University)
## Author(Jessie Oehrlein)
## MLT(stat-des-mean-stdev)
## Level(3)
## KEYWORDS('mean', 'standard deviation')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',  'PGML.pl',
    'parserPopUp.pl', 'PGstatisticsmacros.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

Context('Numeric');

$n = random(5, 7);

$opt = random(0, 1);

@dat  = map { random(25, 100) } (1 .. $n);
$mean = stats_mean(@dat);

$K = $opt == 0 ? min(@dat) - random(1, 5) : int($mean);

$dd = DropDown([ 'increase', 'decrease', 'stays the same' ], $opt);

BEGIN_PGML
  
Consider the following data set.  Find the mean.  

Data set: [@ join ", ", @dat; @]*

a) Find the mean: [_]{$mean}{10} 

b) If the data value [$K] was added to the set, the standard deviation would [_]{$dd}

*You do not need to calculate the standard deviation to answer this question.* 
END_PGML

$sum = 0;
$sum += $_ for (@dat);

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
a) for the mean, we use the sum of the data values divided by the number of points or 
[```\frac{[$sum]}{[$n]} = [@ Round($mean,3) @]```]

b) Note that in this case, the new value is [@ $opt == 0 ? 'less than the minimum, therefore the spread of the data increases' : 'very close to the mean, so the dataset is more concentrated to the middle' @]. Therefore the standard deviation would [$dd].
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
